  "worked_solution": "In this situation, we want to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\pi  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi r \\\\ 12\\pi  & =6\\pi r \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius at that moment is $2\\text{\u00a0m}.$",
